Structural design patterns are all about how classes and objects are composed to form larger structures to enable flexibility, extensibility, and clean architecture.

Here are some deep-rooted Structural Design Patterns working silently behind the scenes in the Spring framework:


✅ 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝘅𝘆 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻: Provide a placeholder for another object to control access to it

 • 𝘚𝘱𝘳𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘈𝘖𝘗 (𝘈𝘴𝘱𝘦𝘤𝘵-𝘖𝘳𝘪𝘦𝘯𝘵𝘦𝘥 𝘗𝘳𝘰𝘨𝘳𝘢𝘮𝘮𝘪𝘯𝘨) and 𝘵𝘳𝘢𝘯𝘴𝘢𝘤𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯𝘢𝘭 support are heavily dependent on the Proxy pattern.

 • Behind that simple annotation is a dynamically created proxy managing cross-cutting concerns like logging, security, and transactions.


✅ 𝗗𝗲𝗰𝗼𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗼𝗿 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻: Attach additional responsibilities to an object dynamically

 • Used in the BeanPostProcessor.

 • Through 𝘉𝘦𝘢𝘯𝘗𝘰𝘴𝘵𝘗𝘳𝘰𝘤𝘦𝘴𝘴𝘰𝘳, Spring dynamically enhances beans without modifying the original logic.


✅ 𝗔𝗱𝗮𝗽𝘁𝗲𝗿 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻: Convert the interface of a class into another interface clients expect

 • 𝘚𝘱𝘳𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘔𝘝𝘊 uses 𝘏𝘢𝘯𝘥𝘭𝘦𝘳𝘈𝘥𝘢𝘱𝘵𝘦𝘳 to call different types of controllers (like @Controller, HttpRequestHandler, etc.) in a uniform way.

 • It's the glue that keeps the dispatcher decoupled from controller implementations.


✅ 𝗙𝗮𝗰𝗮𝗱𝗲 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻: Provide a simplified interface to a complex subsystem

 • Spring provides simplified interfaces for many subsystems (ORM, Security, Messaging) using the Facade pattern.

 • 𝘑𝘥𝘣𝘤𝘛𝘦𝘮𝘱𝘭𝘢𝘵𝘦 and 𝘙𝘦𝘴𝘵𝘛𝘦𝘮𝘱𝘭𝘢𝘵𝘦 are perfect examples—wrapping complex logic like connection handling or HTTP calls behind a clean, developer-friendly API.


✅ 𝗖𝗼𝗺𝗽𝗼𝘀𝗶𝘁𝗲 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻: Compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies

 • Used in 𝘚𝘱𝘳𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘚𝘦𝘤𝘶𝘳𝘪𝘵𝘺 and 𝘚𝘱𝘳𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘌𝘹𝘱𝘳𝘦𝘴𝘴𝘪𝘰𝘯 𝘓𝘢𝘯𝘨𝘶𝘢𝘨𝘦 (SpEL).

 • Seen in the internals of SpEL (Spring Expression Language) and Spring Security, where complex expressions and access rules are modeled as trees of evaluable nodes.


💡 Next time you're annotating a method or wiring a bean, remember: you're not just writing config—you're harnessing time-tested patterns applied at framework scale.

